**AMENDED 2026-09-05 — ITEM D's 127x AND ITS "half of the port's p6 cost"
ARE STALE.** compute_stats is 1.349x C per call and the LR search is
3.2-7.7 % of the port's p6 frame on the four contents below; the p6 port/C
ratios are 2.30-2.75x. See item D's amendment and
`benchmarks/compute_stats_x86_recheck_2026-09-05.meta`. Every other finding
in this record is un-rechecked and stands as written.

CALL-COUNT ATTRIBUTION ON REAL CONTENT: does any edge that read ~1.0x on the
synthetic gradient read >1.5x on textured photos, a screenshot, or line art?
Answer: NO for every MD-search edge the three CPU fixes touched — the tx-type
promotion rule, the MDS1 skip and the per-stage candidate counts are exact or
within 2 % on every byte-identical real-content cell. What real content DOES
change: the per-trial residual re-derivation grows from 1.38x to 2.14x, the
allocator from 1,339x to 9,292x, and two kernels that are 1:1 by count but
12x and 127x per call (CFL predict, Wiener compute_stats) become visible —
the second one is CONTENT-INDEPENDENT and is half of the port's p6 cost on
every image, gradient included. Two screen-content cells at p2 are NOT
byte-identical (a correctness finding, reported separately, not counted).

